                <pb facs="0129" n="31" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg31"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/11180: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-09-10T17:00:00+08:00">September 10, 1930—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:43 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>799. Following from the American Consul [General] at Mukden: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“September 9, 10 a.m. Reliably informed that Wellington Koo now here told
                            the Minister of Foreign Affairs at Peking that he ‘wished to <pb
                                facs="0130" n="32" xml:id="pg32"/> see termination of civil war
                            [and] settlement of the political disputes by peaceful means, if need be
                            through mediation or good offices of Mukden’. He is said to have
                            suggested convocation of a people’s assembly. Apparently he will not
                            join Government. The trend of events seems to be toward a peaceful
                            settlement of the war.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Announced [<hi rendition="#italic">Also?</hi>]: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“September 10, 11 a.m. Northeastern Political Council now assembled
                            Mukden to reconsider attitude of Manchuria toward civil war. Reliably
                            informed that peaceful occupation of Tientsin-Peiping area by Mukden
                            forces in interest of Nanking is favored in high quarters in belief that
                            it will hasten end of war. Yen’s delegates made further offers of high
                            positions to Mukden yesterday. Mukden’s attitude expected to be decided
                            shortly.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
